4.4. Strain data recovery
Fig. 8 illustrates the recovered strain data of the CFST shrinkage specimen in Stage I. In the situations of the 30 % loss and the 50 % loss, the recovered strain data are consistent with the real strain. As for the 70 % loss case, the recovered strain data is still in good agreement with the real data, although the difference between them is relatively larger than that in the former two cases as expected. The strain difference within the block of 25th — 40th days is mainly due to the accumulated estimation error caused by the lack of updates. Fig. 9 illustrates the comparison between the recovered strain and the real strain of the CFST-creep specimen within Stage I. It is seen that the significant strain variation during the loading period at the 15th day were missing. The loading-induced strain is elastic strain, hence it is estimated via Eq. (2) based on the known loading information. The recovered strain data are consistent with the real data for most of the time, whereas estimation error during the loading period is relatively larger. This is because the abrupt loading-induced strain decrease leads to an abrupt change to the covariance matrix in the IMM-KF, resulting in prediction error. Besides, the predicted error strain accumulates with time due to the lack of modification within the missing zone. The accumulated error is modified by the proposed IMM-KF algorithm when new strain data is available at the end of the missing zone. Table 2 shows the RMSEs and the NRMSEs of the four specimens during Stage I. With the loss ratio increases from 30 % to 70 %, the RMSEs of the CFST-shrinkage specimen gradually increases from 0.9 με to 2.9 με. Accordingly, the NRMSE increases from 2.4 % to 8.6 %. The RMSEs of the CFST-creep specimen indicate a slight growth from 3.5 με to 3.9 με as the loss ratio increases, indicating a minor rise of the NRMSEs from 2.1 % to 2.4 %, manifesting a high accuracy. In addition, the NRMSE of the concrete-shrinkage specimen is under 10 % if 